**Read:** The complete method behind one electrical, thermal, optical and magnetic measurement from [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), [Electrical, Optical, and Magnetic Properties of Materials — MIT 3.23](../sources/Functional-Materials-MIT-3-23.lingua#r_3TPN5CK13R844S7PGFK7C1STVN), [Electron interactions, magnetism and superconductivity — Daniel C. Ralph](../sources/Interacting-Electrons-Magnetism-Superconductivity-Ralph.lingua#r_3B28YDT2Z6KYBRZ8DHM1069TW1) and the existing electromagnetic NIST source. Use their primary references where an experiment's details are only summarized.

Separate excitation, specimen state, transducer response, calibration, forward model and parameter inference. Four-probe measurements can reduce certain contact contributions without eliminating every geometry or inhomogeneity effect. Optical inversion can trade thickness against constants; magnetic inference can trade internal-field correction against susceptibility; thermal fitting can trade interface conductance against film conductivity.

**Work:** For each method draw the measurement chain and write an observable before naming a material property. Compare candidate models under changed conditions and report unresolved parameters. Maintain actual evidence separately from simulated curves and proposed experiments.

Use repeatability, independently measured dimensions, uncertainty propagation, withheld data and mechanism-specific observations. A numerically precise fit is not automatically a physically unique explanation. The advanced studies now join these measurement choices to the complete readings.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
